  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some showers will again be possible this evening. \n\nAn area of showers associated with the deformation band will wrap \nback in across WNY this evening. This will provide yet another \nchance for some light measurable precipitation, with no more than a \ntenth of an inch in spots overnight. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next round of rain showers arrives late Monday \nnight with strong thunderstorms possible across western NY Tuesday.\n\nThe next low pressure system will pass east across the Great Lakes \nlate Monday and into Tuesday, pushing a cold front southeast across \nthe region Tuesday evening. Ahead of the front and aligned with a \nleading shortwave, expect another round of rain showers Tuesday from \nthe Niagara Frontier east towards the North Country. A few \nthunderstorms can't be ruled out as mid-level lapse rates approach 7 \nC/km with the presence of an EML and effective shear of 35-40 kt \nthanks to a 50 kt jet at 700mb. \n\nWhile there still remains quite a bit of uncertainty with high \ntemperatures across the region Tuesday, latest ensemble trends have \nshifted the track of the low slightly farther north. This will allow \nportions of western NY to remain under better warm southwesterly \nflow as 850mb temps climb around 14-16 degC, near the climatological \n99th percentile for mid-October. This could support temperatures 10 \ndegF above normal, especially across the Southern Tier. NBM shows \nTuesday afternoon highs for locations north of I-90 with \nconsiderable spread still mainly tied to the timing of \nprecipitation, cloud cover, and the frontal passage. \n\nThis being said, storms will likely remain elevated due to poor low-\nlevel lapse rates, but additional shifts in the low track could \nprovide more limited cloud cover improving boundary layer \ndestabilization and potential for afternoon thunderstorm development \nover western NY. SPC has highlighted western NY with a Day 3 \nMarginal Risk for severe storms.",
